# Split-Bank Electro

**Ingredients:** CAMEROONIAN MVET + DETROIT ELECTRO + PREPARED CLAVINET + CALABASH / WOODBLOCK + BASS CLARINET + SHORT ANALOG BASS

**Creative Catalyst:** The genre began during a dry-season residency in a decommissioned cocoa-weighing depot outside Yaoundé, where mvet player Nadège Essomba was testing a concert-adapted stick-zither whose strings were grouped on opposite sides of a central bridge. Detroit producer Marcus Hale was working in the same building with a compact drum machine, mono sequencer, and several skeletal electro patterns. His first instinct was to sample one complete mvet phrase and loop it. Nadège instead isolated two complementary string banks: one side carried low anchors and repeated dyads, while the opposite side carried short answering figures. Marcus split the electronic arrangement in the same way. Kick, bass, and low toms answered one bank; snare, claves, synth stabs, and upper percussion answered the other. Prepared clavinet, calabash percussion, woodblock, bass clarinet, and short analog bass completed a form where the bridge of one acoustic instrument becomes the dividing line for the entire ensemble.

Split-Bank Electro is a taut acoustic-electronic dance form built from mvet string geometry and Detroit electro. It usually sits between 126 and 136 BPM in 4/4, driven by compact syncopated kick, dry snare, clipped handclap, short tom cells, metallic ticks, restrained sixteenth hats, and a short mono bass sequence. The mvet provides the compositional map. Instead of functioning as a decorative plucked lead, its strings are divided into two functional banks whose alternating attacks determine orchestration, register, stereo weight, and rhythmic permission.

The mvet is recorded with one small-diaphragm condenser aimed toward each string bank, one ribbon microphone near the central resonating body for woody midrange, and one restrained room pair. The two bank microphones remain discrete. They are not hard-panned to extremes; each occupies a stable but natural position so hand travel and call-and-response remain audible without becoming a stereo gimmick. The body microphone stays centered and low in the mix, adding shared resonance between the banks.

Players favor compact pentatonic and modal figures, tonic-fifth anchors, octave jumps, repeated minor-third turns, fourths, suspended seconds, and two- to seven-note cells. Continuous virtuoso runs are avoided. The defining language comes from alternation: a phrase on one side establishes a proposition and the opposite side either answers, withholds, compresses, or redirects it.

The central compositional device is the **Bank Split**. The mvet is treated as two coordinated rhythmic voices designated BANK A and BANK B. BANK A normally carries lower or more stable pitch material; BANK B carries shorter replies, upper-register fragments, or syncopated interruptions. Every electronic instrument is assigned a default allegiance to one bank. A BANK A attack may authorize kick, bass, or low tom. A BANK B attack may authorize clap, high percussion, clavinet, or a short synth stab. The assignments can change between sections but must remain stable long enough for the listener to infer the logic.

A second device, **Bridge Transfer**, moves one recurring musical figure from one string bank to the other without changing its basic rhythm. When the figure crosses the bridge, the electronic arrangement also changes ownership. A pattern that previously triggered bass may begin triggering snare or clavinet once it appears from the opposite bank. The melody therefore stays recognizable while its rhythmic consequences change.

A third device, **Cross-Bank Lock**, occurs when both hands land near-simultaneous attacks on opposing banks. These paired strikes authorize the strongest full-ensemble events: combined kick and clap, bass-plus-clavinet accents, or a short bass-clarinet stab. Cross-Bank Lock is deliberately rare so it functions like a structural exclamation mark rather than a constant backbeat.

A fourth device, **Single-Side Quarantine**, restricts the mvet to one bank for four, eight, or sixteen bars. All electronic events belonging to the silent bank are removed as well. The result is a partial groove that feels mechanically incomplete without using a conventional breakdown. When the second bank returns, its associated electronic family returns with it.

A fifth device, **Bridge Toll**, requires a short silence before a major phrase transfers from one side to the other. The silence can be one sixteenth, one eighth, or one quarter note depending on tempo. No riser or fill is allowed to occupy the gap. The absence makes the transfer audible as a physical crossing rather than an ordinary variation.

Detroit-electro drums remain crisp, compact, and unsentimental. Kick is punchy and short rather than sub-heavy. Snare is dry and often reinforced by one clipped clap. Toms use small syncopated figures. Hats are restrained, with brief sixteenth runs interrupted by deliberate gaps. Cowbell or metallic tick can appear sparingly. The groove should suggest machine precision without flattening the microtiming of the mvet.

The short analog bass uses root, fifth, octave, flat seventh, and occasional chromatic approach notes with fast release. It is normally allied with BANK A, but a Bridge Transfer can temporarily move its authorization to BANK B. Bass patterns remain one or two bars long and avoid long slides. The low end should punch, stop, and leave room for the central resonator.

Prepared clavinet provides dry upper-mid punctuation. Selected strings are damped with removable felt or paper. Parts favor open fifths, suspended seconds, minor sevenths, octave fragments, dead-note sixteenths, and clipped two-note dyads. The clavinet usually belongs to BANK B and becomes more active when the mvet's answering figures are sparse.

Calabash percussion provides hollow acoustic weight through fingertip taps, muted palm strikes, shell knocks, and short rubbed textures. Woodblock supplies a narrow transient for selected Bridge Transfers. These sounds replace individual machine events instead of forming a continuous parallel percussion layer.

Bass clarinet supplies low acoustic counterweight through chalumeau pedals, tongue stops, breath-only attacks, falling minor seconds, and short overblown edges. It appears mainly during Single-Side Quarantine when one electronic family has disappeared. Its role is to occupy temporary gaps without restoring the missing bank's rhythmic function.

Vocals are optional and sparse. Ewondo, French, or English fragments may reference weights, divided ledgers, bridge numbers, cocoa lots, mirrored columns, or instructions such as “left bank holds,” “cross the bridge,” “one side only,” and “both hands now.” Delivery should be spoken, clipped, vocoder-tinted only in isolated phrases, or low chanted. Full pop choruses are avoided.

Effects remain disciplined. Mvet stays mostly dry with a short room reflection. Clavinet receives a tiny spring or plate. Bass clarinet gets a dark chamber. Analog bass may use mild saturation. Electro percussion can use very short gated ambience, but long dub echoes, shimmer, wide chorus, EDM risers, and huge reverbs are excluded. The principal sense of movement must come from which string bank is active, not from automated stereo effects.

The defining tension is bilateral acoustic phrasing against centralized machine logic. One instrument behaves like two cooperating players, and the entire electronic arrangement has to choose which side it is listening to.

## Genre DNA

**Tempo & Pulse:** 126–136 BPM, primarily 4/4 Detroit electro with compact syncopated kick, dry snare/clap, short tom cells, restrained sixteenth hats, metallic ticks, short mono bass, and alternating mvet attacks divided between two functional string banks.

**Harmonic Palette:** Pentatonic and modal centers, tonic-fifth anchors, suspended seconds, minor thirds, open fourths/fifths, minor sevenths, octave reinforcement, static one- or two-chord fields, and short chromatic bass approaches.

**Production Signatures:**

1. **Bank Split** — mvet strings are divided into BANK A and BANK B, each tied to a different family of electronic responses.
2. **Bridge Transfer** — a recurring figure moves from one string bank to the other and changes which electronic events it authorizes.
3. **Cross-Bank Lock** — near-simultaneous attacks from opposing banks trigger rare full-ensemble accents.
4. **Single-Side Quarantine** — one mvet bank and its associated electronic family disappear together for a fixed section.
5. **Bridge Toll** — a short compulsory silence precedes major cross-bank transfers.
6. **No-Sample-Loop Rule** — the core mvet part must remain a continuous live two-bank performance; one finished phrase cannot be reduced to a repeated decorative loop.

**Dynamics:** Intensity grows through faster bank alternation, shorter Bridge Tolls, denser Cross-Bank Locks, firmer calabash strikes, brighter clavinet punctuation, and more active electro tom cells. Peaks remain dry, agile, and rhythmically architectural rather than massive.

# Scene & Origin Lore

Split-Bank Electro formed inside a retired cocoa grading and weighing depot on the industrial edge of Yaoundé. The building retained mechanical scales, numbered sack bays, brass counterweights, handwritten crop ledgers, inspection tables, burlap stacks, ventilation shutters, red stencil marks, and long concrete aisles divided by a central yellow floor line.

Mvet player **Nadège Essomba** had been using the weighing hall because its dry acoustics preserved the distinction between individual strings and the shared resonating body. She was developing a performance method that treated the two functional string banks as independent but interdependent voices.

Detroit producer **Marcus Hale** arrived with a compact electro setup built around drum machine, mono sequencer, and a small analog bass synth. His early sketch placed a sampled mvet phrase over a finished electro beat. Nadège rejected the approach because the instrument's physical division had become invisible. She performed the same cell first on one bank, then on the other. Marcus noticed that the transfer sounded like a change of speaker even before any electronic processing was applied. He reassigned half the drum machine to one bank and half to the other.

Prepared-clavinet player **Élise Mballa** joined after developing terse upper-register replies that could mirror BANK B without doubling it. Percussionist **Samuel Nkoa** added calabash and woodblock as acoustic substitutes for selected drum-machine hits. Bass-clarinetist **Darius Cole** completed the ensemble with low air and reed pressure for sections where Single-Side Quarantine removed part of the electronic floor.

Their first performances were called **split ledgers**. A large grading board behind the band was divided into two columns labeled A and B. Before each piece, Marcus wrote a handful of symbols beside each column: KICK, BASS, CLAP, KEY, SILENCE. The board controlled nothing electronically. It simply showed the current allegiance of the machine voices to the two sides of the mvet.

The visual language developed around cocoa brown, calabash amber, ledger cream, oxidized brass, weighing-scale green, stencil red, graphite black, and faded industrial yellow. The unofficial rule is that if the track sounds structurally identical after collapsing the mvet into one mono loop, the arrangement has failed.

