## Track 1: Lot 31 Crosses the Yellow Line

**Story & Context:** At 132 BPM, Nadège opens with a six-note BANK A figure built from tonic, fifth, octave, minor third, fifth, and tonic. Marcus assigns kick and short analog bass to that bank while BANK B remains nearly silent. After eight bars, the same six-note rhythm undergoes Bridge Transfer and is played from BANK B in a higher register. The bass immediately disappears; clap, prepared clavinet, and one metallic tick become the responding family instead. Samuel marks the crossing with a single woodblock after a one-eighth-note Bridge Toll. Darius enters only after the transfer, using one low chalumeau pedal under the now bass-light section. The first Cross-Bank Lock arrives near the midpoint, when both hands strike opposing tonic registers together and the full electro kit appears for one beat.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Cocoa inspection ledger stamped LOT 31, a faded yellow warehouse floor line splitting the page into two columns, two mvet string diagrams crossing the line, one brass scale weight placed exactly at the midpoint. Sparse lyric fragments limited to “lot thirty-one,” “cross the line,” “bank B takes it,” and “both hands now.”

## Track 2: Column B Withholds the Clap

**Story & Context:** At 136 BPM, BANK B carries the principal syncopated figure, but Marcus removes its usual clap response through an extended Single-Side Quarantine. The track therefore keeps kick, bass, BANK A low plucks, calabash, and bass clarinet while all BANK B-associated electronics remain absent. Élise is also silent because clavinet belongs to that side. Nadège continues playing short BANK B attacks acoustically, making the missing electronic replies increasingly obvious. After sixteen bars, a Bridge Toll of one quarter note interrupts the groove. The quarantine ends with three fast Cross-Bank Locks; clap, clavinet, high tom, and metallic tick return simultaneously but remain active for only four bars before the texture contracts again.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Two-column cocoa ledger with COLUMN B outlined in red but several expected checkmarks left blank, one clap symbol crossed out, calabash shell beside a mechanical counter, dark mvet strings stretching diagonally across the form. Sparse lyric fragments limited to “column B,” “withhold the clap,” “one side only,” and “return four bars.”

## Track 3: Both Scales Balance After Closing

**Story & Context:** The closer runs at 126 BPM and begins with the two banks in strict alternation: one low BANK A pluck, one upper BANK B reply, one bar of minimal electro, then silence. Marcus keeps the bass extremely short and allows no full drum pattern for the first two minutes. Nadège gradually shortens the delay between opposing banks until the alternation becomes a nearly continuous interlock. Élise answers only every fourth BANK B phrase with a dry open-fifth clavinet dyad. Samuel uses soft calabash palm strokes beneath BANK A and woodblock only at major Bridge Transfers. In the final minute, both banks settle into equal rhythmic density. Cross-Bank Locks appear at the end of each four-bar cycle, but instead of growing louder the electronics progressively disappear. The last passage contains mvet alone, alternating perfectly balanced banks around the central bridge. The EP ends with one shared body resonance and no drum-machine cadence.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Empty cocoa-weighing depot after closing, two mechanical scales resting at equal zero points on opposite sides of a yellow floor line, one mvet centered between them, late orange light across burlap dust. Sparse lyric fragments limited to “both scales,” “balance after closing,” “left answers right,” and “no final machine.”

